      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a stockpot over medium heat. Add the onion and cook for 8 minutes until soft and golden."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the garlic, cumin, turmeric and chilli flakes. Stir for 1 minute. Add the tomatoes and cook for 5 minutes until broken down."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the drained kidney beans and water. Bring to the boil, then reduce to a steady simmer."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Stir in the polenta or maize grits. Cook uncovered over low-medium heat for 60 minutes, stirring occasionally. The maize will absorb liquid and thicken the stew."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The stew is ready when it is thick and the maize is completely soft. Add more water during cooking if it becomes too dry before the maize is tender. Serve with ugali or chapati."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Maize is still hard after an hour"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add more water and continue cooking. Coarse dried maize can take up to 90 minutes. Using pre-soaked dried maize shortens this. Polenta will cook much faster."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Stew is too thin after the full cooking time"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Crush some of the beans against the side of the pot with the back of a spoon. This releases starch and thickens the stew naturally."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Flavour is bland"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Increase the cumin and add more chilli. Taste after an hour of cooking and adjust. Kidney beans need generous seasoning to taste good."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Nyayo Beans were served in Kenyan schools during the 1980s as part of a national nutrition programme. The name comes from the Swahili word for footsteps, part of a political slogan promoting unity. The dish became associated with everyday life rather than politics. It is now simply home food. Beans and maize together provide a complete protein, which is why they have been paired across African and Latin American cooking for centuries."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
